WEBVTT

00:00.360 --> 00:02.400
So let's go ahead with question one.

00:02.400 --> 00:07.470
So an enterprise has a large number of accounts owned by a separate business group.

00:07.860 --> 00:12.960
One of the accounts was recently compromised and the attacker launched a large number of instances,

00:12.960 --> 00:15.020
resulting in the high bill for that account.

00:15.030 --> 00:20.880
So, by the way, that is a very common attack against those accounts to start a large number of instances.

00:20.880 --> 00:23.430
And then, for example, mine for bitcoins.

00:23.850 --> 00:24.300
Okay.

00:24.330 --> 00:29.490
The security breach was addressed, but management has asked the solution architect to develop a solution

00:29.490 --> 00:32.700
to prevent excessive spending in all accounts.

00:32.970 --> 00:36.960
Each business group wants to retain full control over its accounts.

00:37.410 --> 00:41.880
Which solution should the solutions architect recommend to meet these requirements?

00:42.300 --> 00:49.020
So here, the important bits of that question is that, number one, we want to prevent excessive spending.

00:49.020 --> 00:53.350
And number two, we want to retain full control over the account.

00:53.370 --> 00:55.440
There are two important things about this question.

00:55.530 --> 00:57.450
So let's look at the architecture.

00:57.450 --> 01:02.880
So we have three areas account in this example and then we launch various types of 82 instances.

01:02.880 --> 01:08.370
So tw to see 5x1e and this is also the case in another account, so we're pretty free.

01:08.370 --> 01:14.370
But one of these accounts was attacked and there were a lot of instances launched into that accounts

01:14.370 --> 01:17.670
and this was the problem with excessive billing.

01:17.670 --> 01:23.730
So we want to be able to prevent that problem while making sure all these accounts still have control

01:23.730 --> 01:25.890
over the type of stuff they do.

01:26.040 --> 01:33.150
So Option A is to use organizations to add address accounts to the master accounts and then create a

01:33.150 --> 01:39.870
service control policy SEP that uses the EC2 instance type condition key to prevent the launch of high

01:39.870 --> 01:42.510
cost instances in the accounts.

01:42.510 --> 01:44.130
So let's have a look again.

01:44.130 --> 01:50.760
So we have this account and now we have created an organization and we have added all the accounts into

01:50.760 --> 01:51.960
the organization.

01:52.380 --> 01:54.030
Now we also have an SEP.

01:54.030 --> 02:00.690
So service control policy and this is helpful to prevent at the account level some API calls from being

02:00.690 --> 02:01.130
made.

02:01.140 --> 02:01.610
Okay.

02:01.710 --> 02:08.610
So here the SEP is defined at the organization level and it is applied to each and every single address

02:08.610 --> 02:09.150
accounts.

02:09.270 --> 02:15.090
But this SEP what it is that restricts us from launching high cost instance types.

02:15.090 --> 02:21.420
So that means that all of these x1e instances that are not authorized to be launched because they are

02:21.420 --> 02:22.530
too expensive.

02:22.800 --> 02:28.710
But in that case, even though that would work in preventing the bill from being high, we also preventing

02:28.710 --> 02:33.930
the accounts from operating normally because for example, account one and Account three in this example

02:33.960 --> 02:38.160
still would need to launch that very expensive instance type if there was a use case for it.

02:38.160 --> 02:44.580
So even though that helps us prevent the billing, it does not give us full control over our accounts.

02:44.580 --> 02:46.680
And so we have to let go of this option.

02:47.430 --> 02:54.570
Option B is to use an IAM policy into an IM group in each account, and we use the same condition as

02:54.570 --> 02:55.140
before.

02:55.170 --> 02:57.720
And then we place all the IAM users in each group.

02:57.780 --> 03:04.890
So we have these accounts and now this time we put users in a group and then we add a policy and item

03:04.890 --> 03:10.590
policy to the group and all of a sudden, same as before, we cannot launch these instances and we do

03:10.590 --> 03:12.420
the exact same thing in the other accounts.

03:12.510 --> 03:19.590
So this is a problem because again, we are preventing our users from operating normally into each account

03:19.590 --> 03:22.260
so they don't retain full control over the accounts.

03:22.440 --> 03:28.380
But also this could be a problem because now a tricky user could have a role and the role would not

03:28.380 --> 03:29.760
have a policy attached to it.

03:29.760 --> 03:35.250
And the user, by assuming the role, would probably be able to still launch some accounts, some,

03:35.250 --> 03:37.710
um, instances that are high cost.

03:37.710 --> 03:42.210
Just a little tip here, but it doesn't matter for this question, just something I want to give you

03:42.210 --> 03:43.290
an insight into.

03:43.290 --> 03:48.570
So again, this option right now, it does not work because we don't have control over our accounts.

03:48.570 --> 03:55.260
And personally, I would prefer to use an STP and an organization than using users in a group and attached

03:55.260 --> 03:55.890
policy.

03:56.910 --> 04:00.780
Option C Enable building alerts on each of account accounts.

04:00.780 --> 04:07.020
Create an Amazon Cloud Watch alarm that sends an SMS notification to the account administrator whenever

04:07.020 --> 04:09.600
their accounts exceeds the spending budget.

04:09.720 --> 04:11.700
So very simple same architecture.

04:11.700 --> 04:16.710
But now we create a billing alarm, which is a cloud watch metric alongside an alarm.

04:16.710 --> 04:22.410
And this alarm, because it is a cloud watch alarm, can send notifications into an SMS topic and we

04:22.410 --> 04:24.900
would apply the exact same into every account.

04:24.960 --> 04:31.080
So here, as soon as the billing alarm goes over, is a threshold that we would define, for example,

04:31.080 --> 04:38.370
the average spending, then we would receive an SMS notification and if the administrator is up, then

04:38.370 --> 04:42.150
they can go ahead and look into the account and see what's happening.

04:42.630 --> 04:48.810
Or if you wanted to automate stuff, we could have the less nice notification trigger a lambda function

04:48.810 --> 04:52.800
that would automate some of the tasks that the administrator would do to look at the billing.

04:52.800 --> 04:54.690
So this looks like a feasible option.

04:54.720 --> 04:57.150
Just note that enabled us.

04:57.150 --> 04:59.070
You cannot limit the spending.

04:59.070 --> 04:59.970
You can only create a.

05:00.060 --> 05:03.690
Building alarms and then you would receive notifications based on that.

05:03.690 --> 05:08.520
But you cannot say to the recipients you cannot spend more than $500 each month.

05:08.520 --> 05:11.310
That does not work and that is not an option in us.

05:11.400 --> 05:13.620
So this option C is something I really like.

05:13.620 --> 05:19.650
So we'll keep it in mind and then option Z enable address cost explorer in each account and regularly

05:19.650 --> 05:24.930
review the customer reports for each account to ensure spending does not exceed the planned budgets.

05:24.930 --> 05:27.480
So we would enable cost explorer in each of accounts.

05:27.480 --> 05:30.030
So Cost Explorer is a dash boarding technology.

05:30.060 --> 05:33.030
It uses the same underlying data as the billing alarm.

05:33.030 --> 05:38.490
But the difference is the billing alarm is going to send a notification to you, so you are going to

05:38.490 --> 05:40.260
be alerted as soon as possible.

05:40.260 --> 05:46.200
But with the cost explorer, the administrator has to go into each account and then explore the reports

05:46.200 --> 05:47.760
and then find anomalies.

05:47.760 --> 05:53.760
So in this example, it's going to take a lot longer to find any spending anomalies and any attacks

05:53.760 --> 05:55.350
happening in your in our accounts.

05:55.350 --> 06:00.840
And so therefore, Cost Explorer is not something that we would be looking into.

06:02.160 --> 06:06.840
Lastly, I just want to offer so option C is obviously the right answer in this question, but I just

06:06.840 --> 06:10.050
want to offer some more insights to what could be done in a real world scenario.

06:10.050 --> 06:16.020
So if you know, for example, that your instances are being launched, you can use limits to make sure

06:16.020 --> 06:20.520
that these high cost instances are not launched at greater scale.

06:20.880 --> 06:25.370
You could also create a lambda function that would be triggered pretty often to generate a report of

06:25.410 --> 06:29.130
to how many instances of each type are launched and if there is any anomaly.

06:29.130 --> 06:30.450
So there's a bunch of things you could do.

06:30.450 --> 06:34.440
Obviously they are not answers in this question, but it could be a way for you to control your spending

06:34.440 --> 06:35.340
on to a loss.

06:35.340 --> 06:36.930
And that is such an important topic.

06:36.930 --> 06:41.640
So please make sure to review your cost once in a while, understand what's happening in your account

06:41.640 --> 06:45.900
and make sure to create these billing alarms to be alerted in case anything goes wrong.

06:45.900 --> 06:46.380
All right.

06:46.380 --> 06:46.950
That's it.

06:46.950 --> 06:48.660
